  "strategy": "**\n\n### 1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe previous approach focused on fixed-length sequences and raid by alternating linearization and subgradient descent. My new approach will explore sequences of varying lengths, use a more sophisticated optimization technique, and incorporate adaptive M tightening.\n\nThe objective is to minimize `2*n*max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)**2`. We need a sequence with non-negative floats that doesn't contain NaNs or Infs and has a sum at least 0.01.\n\nWe will explore sequences from length 50 up to 2500 (with fallback to 100-length if needed). This range provides better coverage than the fixed 100-1500 of the parent.\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification\n\nWe define:\n- A normalized sequence space where the sum of coefficients equals 1. The actual objective then is `2*n*max(convolve(a,a))`.\n- The cross-correlation terms:\n  - For each position, we compute the coefficient and cross-terms based on neighboring indices\n  - The outer indices are truncated to avoid out-of-bound values\n\nThe constraints are:\n- `a[i]` must be positive for all i\n- The coefficient dimensions must not vary beyond the allowed space\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure\n\nWe'll implement:\na) `normalized_init(seq_len)`: Generates a normalized sequence with length `seq_len`\nb) `convolve(seq)`: Computes full convolution using FFT\nc) `update_sequence(prev, new, blend)`: Updates the sequence based on the blended approach\n\nThe main optimization loop will:\n- Start with a sequence of length 50\n- Iterate through increasing lengths up to 2500\n- Use blend factors `.05` to `.08` for transition consistency\n- Include fallback to the parent method if normalization fails\n- Terminate when an improvement threshold is reached\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Variations\n\nWe'll:\n- Iterate sequence lengths from 50 to 2500\n- Use significance factors for stability:\n  1)`0.05` = `.1` (.needy)\n  2)`0.02` = `.15` (.risky)\n  3)`0.07` (loading point)\n\nStopping criteria could be:\n- Time exceeding parameters\n- Consecutive inversion steps\n- Performance plateaus\n\n### 5. Computation Budget\n\nWe allocate:\n- Initial performance testing for lengths `<=100`\n- Main budget allocation for lengths `<=500`\n- Ensure fallback to stable neighbors is possible\n- With generous blend reinitialization\n\nThis totals around 1500 seconds but remains dynamic.\n\n### 6. Validation and Return Contract\n\nValidation checks include:\n- Sequence normalization (sum=1)\n- Convolution achieving finite values\n- Progress monitoring (magnitude checks)\n\nThe final sequence is:\n- Checked with `evaluate_sequence`\n- Thenclipped to [0, 1000] bounds\n- Ifinvalid, deviation from earlier points is tried\n\nThis ensures the solution adheres to the parent method's best practices.",
